WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.664
The live balances view has fifty million rows.

2
00:00:05.664 --> 00:00:10.110
An audit job recomputes the same view from the log, in the warehouse.

3
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.979
The two should match exactly.

4
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:15.690
Somewhere, one balance is off by a single flipped bit.

5
00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:21.759
Comparing row by row means sending all fifty million rows across the network, on every audit.

6
00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:28.819
Instead, each side cuts its keys into ranges, here eight, and hashes all the rows in each range.

7
00:00:28.819 --> 00:00:31.401
If the rows match, the hashes match.

8
00:00:31.401 --> 00:00:34.199
Each box shows both sides, live on top.

9
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:39.457
Then each side hashes pairs of hashes, level by level, up to a single root.

10
00:00:39.457 --> 00:00:40.960
This is a Merkle tree.

11
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:44.670
Now the two sides exchange one hash each: the root.

12
00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.623
They differ, so the views differ somewhere.

13
00:00:47.623 --> 00:00:48.653
One comparison.

14
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:50.935
Compare the root's two children.

15
00:00:50.935 --> 00:00:56.845
The left one matches, so half the ranges are identical, and nothing below it is looked at.

16
00:00:56.845 --> 00:00:58.027
Three comparisons.

17
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.872
Under the right child, the left one matches and the right one differs.

18
00:01:02.872 --> 00:01:03.205
Five.

19
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:07.164
At the bottom, range six differs and range seven matches.

20
00:01:07.164 --> 00:01:10.764
Seven comparisons found the one bad range out of eight.

21
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:16.904
Only now do rows cross the network, and only the rows in range six: an eighth of them.

22
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.250
Each level costs two comparisons.

23
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:28.569
With a thousand and twenty four ranges, the walk takes twenty one comparisons, and leaves about fifty thousand rows to diff, not fifty million.

24
00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:33.987
The cost grows with the number of ranges that differ, not with the size of the view.

25
00:01:33.987 --> 00:01:36.908
If most ranges differ, a plain scan is cheaper.

26
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.715
Exchange hashes, not rows, and go down only where the hashes differ.
